==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}}, from {{etyl|xno|en}} {{term|hidous||lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|hideus|lang=fro}}, from {{term|hisdos|lang=fro}}, from {{term|hisda||lang=fro|horror, fear}} of uncertain origin; confer {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|egidī||horror|lang=goh}}, or {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|hispidosus|lang=la}}, from {{term|hispidus||lang=la|rough, bristly}} <!--fro?: ''[[hidous]], [[hidos]], [[hidus]], [[hisdos]], [[hisdous]]'', {{etyl|fr}} ''[[hideux]]'': confer {{etyl|fro}} ''[[hide]]'', {{term|hisde||fright}}; , {{etyl|en|-}}-->

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈhɪd.iː.ʌs/}} {{SAMPA|/"hId.i:.Vs/}}
* {{audio|en-us-hideous.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Frightful; shocking; extremely [[ugly]].
#: A piteous and hideous spectacle." [[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|Thomas Babington Macaulay]].
# Distressing or offensive to the ear; horrible.
#* '''1719:''' [[w:Daniel Defoe|Daniel Defoe]], ''[[w:Robinson Crusoe|Robinson Crusoe]]''
#*: ''He started up, growling at first, but finding his leg broken, fell down again; and then got upon three legs, and gave the most '''hideous''' roar that ever I heard.''
# Hateful; shocking.
#: ''Sure, you have some hideous matter to deliver.'' - {{Shak.}}

